{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel/kernel.go at exact e66a29775d7050e1158398de0b7e7cbc6f9e4297, two commits over base d1ae6ade (84289545 the code and test checkpoint, e66a2977 the documents) on request/oversized-attachment-disposition (pushed); git merge-tree onto current main 411681f1 is clean (tree bc907edc), and equally clean onto c8c29c69, which 411681f1 descends from. THE REPORTING ARTIFACT for this lane, under promise c2c18651 on request 59154b87 (#22093, the remaining oversized-attachment outcome of #137), delivering the narrowing of decision fcb32a61 (#5075) that Hugh adopted in proposal ddaf3197 (#22135, ratify ecc3db6f). It is the newest artifact on that promise and no separate ready-for-review report is filed.\n\nWHAT CHANGED AT THIS PATH. The ceiling and its enforcement do not move. validateRequestSize is replaced by one measurement and one judgement: measureRequest walks the envelope, the payload and every attachment into a requestSize (envelope, payload, attachment total, count, largest name and size, and a saturated flag), and requestSize.judge spends the ceiling as a remaining budget, envelope then payload then attachments, which is the accounting this kernel has always applied and cannot overflow. addSize saturates instead of wrapping and says whether it had to; a measurement that saturated is refused outright, because its true total is larger than any ceiling a genesis can record. Submit now calls measureRequest on the envelope it has already built, so the envelope is not built twice. requestSize.refusal replaces the bare string with a diagnostic that opens with the same words ('event exceeds genesis ceiling') so every existing caller and document matching that prefix still matches, then adds the measured total, the ceiling, the split as 'envelope N, payload N, attachments N in N files, largest \"name\" at N bytes' (or 'no attachments'), and the remedy: shrink the evidence, split it across several events, or cite a repository path instead of attaching bytes. The largest attachment is settled by name on a size tie, because Go randomises map order and one request must produce one message. boundedName truncates an attachment name to 128 bytes, since the kernel measures a request before the payload tree writer validates names and a raw submission body can carry a key of any length. New exported ValidateUnsignedRequestSize applies that same measurement to an act that is not signed yet: it checks the actor key length, builds a placeholder request whose signature is 64 zero bytes, and runs measureRequest.judge. Because the actor key and the signature are fixed widths and the intent bytes and causal references are already final, that placeholder measures the byte for byte identical envelope the signed request will carry. ValidateRequestSize, the reader re-checks and the genesis descriptor are untouched.\n\nTHE ADOPTED CONDITIONS OF ddaf3197, ONE BY ONE. (1) The hard 1 MiB genesis ceiling and its enforcement are kept exactly: the same requests are refused as before, pinned by TestSummedAccountingRefusesExactlyWhatTheStepwiseAccountingRefused, which keeps the replaced stepwise accounting verbatim as an oracle. (2) The bare refusal is replaced by an actionable diagnostic naming the ceiling, the measured total, the envelope, payload and attachment split, the largest attachment by name and the remedy, pinned at every boundary. (3) Refusal before signing is delivered at the one place that signs, internal/app signRequest, through the kernel's one measurement of a placeholder signed envelope, and observed on each of the three previously unchecked surfaces by counting what reaches the transport or the submission path. (4) The per-surface preflights that the earlier candidate had added to cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go and internal/service/ui.go are removed: all three files are byte for byte identical to d1ae6ade at this head, because one boundary at the signing site covers every surface and three copies of it would be three places to drift. (5) The documents are corrected: the design note's reference-and-locator claim, the gs verify sentence that said only payload sizes are bounded, and limits.md. (6) The census in the packet is replaced by the planner's independent numbers in assert 0a048f5d, attributed to it, and the zero-refusal claim is withdrawn. Retry identity is untouched throughout because nothing re-signs and a refused act mints no signature; each surface test refiles under the same idempotency key and lands.\n\nTHE PLANNER'S #22166 FINDINGS, ANSWERED AT THIS HEAD. (1) Before signing. The finding was correct and the proposal named the wrong API: kernel.ValidateRequestSize verifies an already signed request, so a preflight calling it after BuildActRequest refuses before append, not before signing. The condition is met as adopted and without a second size formula, by exporting the kernel's own measurement over a placeholder-signed envelope and applying it inside signRequest before intent.Sign. The equality is proved rather than asserted, on the verdict and on the refusal text, at the exact ceiling and one byte under, in TestUnsignedMeasurementEqualsTheSignedMeasurement; a mutant that shortens the placeholder signature to zero length fails it. The configured ceiling is read from the workroom, never hard coded. (2) Refused attempts. The zero-refusal claim is withdrawn. limits.md now says that refused admission creates no event, that accepted history therefore cannot show how many attempts were refused or that any were, that no refusal evidence was found in the retained logs that were searched and that the search was limited, and that nobody asking for a larger attachment would appear there either. (3) and (4) Census and the historical comparison. The packet's own script is not published. limits.md carries the planner's independent census, attributed to assert 0a048f5d and dated 2026-09-09: every one of the 27,845 non-genesis events at four pinned heads, records with and without attachments alike, identical on a second run; 555 events carrying 1,772 attachments; nearest-rank p50 1,279, p90 9,145, p99 60,232, maximum 498,733; largest complete event per room Gitseq 196,373 at 5.34 times headroom, Tailapp 535,397 at 1.96, Chess 86,436 at 12.13, Inventory 31,972 at 32.80, against a recorded ceiling of 1,048,576 in all four; largest attachment-free record 74,031. The five-week headroom-loss claim is deleted, and the page says to keep the rooms distinct because the fleet maximum is Tailapp's and is not Gitseq's headroom. (5) Provenance. The three documents carry the commit-1 behaviour artifacts in their front matter (limits.md on kernel e7bd0c2e and app 865529c7, verify.md on kernel e7bd0c2e, architecture.md on all three including intent cc1d53e5), which is why this head is two commits: the behaviour is checkpointed and published first, and the documents cite it. Two further findings came out of the pre-publication review rather than from #22166 and are answered in the same head: an untrusted attachment name must not reach an unbounded error string, and the pre-signing measurement must not turn a workroom whose local record carries no ceiling into one that refuses everything.\n\nOMISSION CONTROLS. Thirteen guards, each neutralised in a scratch copy outside the repository, each run against the test that is supposed to hold it, every final outcome red (evidence/mutants.txt): kernel diagnostic reverted to the bare string; largest-attachment tie-break removed; untrusted attachment name no longer bounded; saturating attachment total no longer refused; placeholder signature shortened to zero length; actor key length no longer checked; signing check moved behind the signature; signing check removed, observed separately at the application boundary, at gs state, at MCP state and at the resident /v0/act; zero-ceiling guard removed; signature measurement never recorded. Two survived on the first attempt and both were fixed rather than argued away. The saturation clause survived because the remaining-budget arithmetic already refused every case the oracle table then held, so the clause was doing nothing observable; the table gained the one shape the arithmetic cannot separate, a saturating attachment total against a wholly unspent maximum ceiling, and the mutant is now red. Moving the check behind the signature survived because the measurement was recorded beside the signing call, so reordering skipped the record too and the counter stayed at zero; the record moved inside the sign helper, so signing and observing signing cannot be separated, and the mutant is now red. Every 'signing check removed' run in the file was repeated after that seam moved, so each recorded outcome is against the delivered source.\n\nGATES. Commit 1, 84289545, on the tree that became it (evidence/gates-commit1.txt): gofmt, go vet, go build, git diff --check all rc 0; go test -count=1 over kernel, intent, app, service, cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp rc 0; go test -race over kernel, app and service rc 0. Two runs came back red, the docset package run and the whole-module run; both were transient, neither was caused by this change, and both are recorded rather than hidden.internal/docset failed once with 'refuse verified frontier rollback: depth 22331 is shorter than previously verified depth 22332': this workroom is live and other actors append while the gates run, so a verified read can see the local frontier marker one position ahead of the head it just read; the immediate rerun passed and internal/docset was green inside the whole-module run, and nothing here touches verification or the frontier marker. TestBatchProcessReadsItsFileAndReportsFailures/positional file failed once inside the whole-module run: it execs the built gs binary with CombinedOutput and parses the result as JSON, and under load the snapshot load passes one second so cmd/gs/main.go prints its progress line into the stream the test parses. That is a pre-existing timing sensitivity in a file this head does not touch, and the isolated rerun of ./cmd/gs/ passed. Commit 2, this head (evidence/gates-commit2.txt): gofmt, go vet, go build, git diff --check, internal/docset and the whole module go test -count=1 ./... all rc 0, with no failures at all.\n\nWHAT IS NOT DONE, AND WHY. The locator path is not implemented: the adopted outcome withdrew it. The genesis ceiling is not raised, which would need a new workroom and is excluded by the request. Reading attachments back through gs inspect is a separate reading-surface outcome and is untouched. The resident's 2 MiB body cap is recorded in limits.md but not changed, and the deliberate asymmetry between batch and merge planning, which measure it themselves, and a single act, which learns from the resident one round trip later, is documented rather than removed. The assessment document itself is not published as a repository file; its corrected figures live in limits.md attributed to the planner's census. No genesis change, migration, external storage, automatic URL access, key operation, resident restart or deployment is part of this head. No UI source changes and the embed is unchanged. Succeeds the live artifacts at this path, including the commit-1 behaviour checkpoint artifact e7bd0c2e at 84289545, which the documents in commit 2 cite as their behaviour basis.","body":{"branch":"request/oversized-attachment-disposition","commit":"e66a29775d7050e1158398de0b7e7cbc6f9e4297","path":"internal/kernel/kernel.go","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:52966895e59050b9a39308e6069ddb9ae7bd0c2e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2c186516fa6f2df92a9b2d23f605c5bf744c388 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0b